{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787155476,"nonce":"r5/4wWPQYbCcLg3O1GNBYHYvrSU=","message":"**DECIDED by the owner, 2026-08-19. Not design (a) from comment #1 — a cheaper shape that makes the same three failures impossible without a visibility mechanism.**\n\n**The ruling: bounded lookback, plus a reconciliation detector.**\n\n```\ndigest =  Cases in (T, T+W]\n       ∪  Cases in (T−L, T]  minus those already reported\n```\n\n`L` is the maximum lateness oto tolerates. Two consequences, and both are the point:\n\n- **Dedupe state only has to span `L`, not forever.** That is the whole saving over comment #1's design and over the per-Case marker table in design (d) — no permanent membership record, no write amplification proportional to history.\n- **`skipped_windows` stops being a backlog.** Nothing is ever owed, because a straggler is picked up by the next digest rather than being missed by a frozen cursor. The number is either deleted or redefined as *\"windows examined and found empty\"* — a diagnostic, not a debt. `893cee4`'s original complaint is answered by removing the concept of a debt, not by fixing the arithmetic.\n\n**Why this beats the `now − safety_lag` cursor** that comment #1 recommended: that design turns a single unmeasurable number into permanent silent loss. A Case is lost exactly when `commit_delay \u003e safety_lag`, and the effective margin is `safety_lag − (reader_clock − writer_clock)`, so inter-pod skew eats the budget invisibly. `b21ba93` (*\"Six tables can 500 when the updating pod's clock lags the creating pod's\"*) is the standing record that this repo already pays that tax. Under bounded lookback, exceeding `L` produces a **duplicate**, not a hole — and a visible duplicate beats an invisible omission.\n\n**Why not the `xid8` visibility watermark**, which is strictly more correct: it needs its own column defaulted from `pg_current_xact_id()`, it stalls behind any long-running transaction (an idle-in-transaction psql session freezes coverage until it closes, then delivers one enormous digest), and it still cannot answer *\"what span does this digest cover\"* — an xid is not a time, so `78388fb` needs a timestamp alongside it regardless. Correctness that costs a second mechanism, for a failure mode the lookback already downgrades to \"counted twice\".\n\n⚠️ **The detector is not optional — it is the half that makes this defensible.** A periodic anti-join for Cases whose event time is older than `now − L` and which appear in no digest. Cheap, runs on a slow cadence, and it converts the residual failure from an invisible hole into a number somebody can alarm on. **Pre-release, the goal is auditable rather than provably correct**: if this does happen, it must be found from a metric and not from a customer.\n\n**Two things to settle while implementing, neither of which changes the shape:**\n\n- **How `L` is chosen and where it lives.** It is not `safety_lag` under another name — the difference is that being wrong about `L` costs a duplicate, so it can be generous. `internal/streaming/service/bridge.go`'s `DefaultLagWindow` is the nearest precedent and lives in `platform/tuning`; the same argument as `DefaultGroupCloseDelay` (`grouping/domain/lifecycle.go:64-73`) applies about a literal versus a reference, and that comment records a real bug from getting it wrong.\n- **Which clock stamps the field the lookback compares against.** ⛔ If it is stamped app-side before the write, `created_at − happened_at` under-reports lateness precisely for the Cases that took longest to commit, because a transaction-scoped `now()` returns transaction START time — the slower the transaction, the further back the stamp lands. Worth confirming before sizing `L`, or `L` is measured against the wrong quantity.\n\n**What this displaces:** comment #1's recommendation (a), and its framing of (d) as \"the most invasive option\". Reframed as *a digest is a bounded-lookback set of Cases rather than a slice of clock*, (d) is the one design that needs no visibility mechanism at all. Comment #1's failure-mode analysis and citations still stand; its conclusion does not.\n\n**Unblocks `78388fb`:** the digest row gains a coverage fact it can render, instead of inferring its span from the policy's *current* `digest_window_s` — which `342e071` shows is unsafe. The honest rendered span is \"since the last digest, plus stragglers\", which is also what an operator probably thought it meant.","files":null}]}